n unattended batch run: no human is present and nobody will answer. Do not ask for confirmation, approval, or review of any draft or plan — launch the team immediately, choose the formal proof pipeline yourself, and keep working until the conjecture is settled. (Array 84835, task 4 stopped after drafting the team prompt and asking the user to confirm launch.)\n112: \n113: The file $F in the current directory defines \\`Candidate\\`, the conjecture\n114:     $STMT\n115: Replace the \\`sorry\\` in exactly ONE of \\`theorem prove_$ID : Candidate\\` or \\`theorem refute_$ID : ¬ Candidate\\`, editing that file in place.\n116: \n117: The invariants are defined in reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean (namespace GraphCalc) — a copy of what Lean actually uses; read it. To find Mathlib lemmas, put #check, example or exact? in a scratch .lean file here and run the checker on it. Because the invariants are computable, \\`decide\\` evaluates them on an explicit small graph built with \\`GraphCalc.ofEdges n [(0, 1), ...]\\` — so a refutation is usually a counterexample graph plus \\`decide\\`, while a proof needs a general argument about all finite graphs.\n118: \n119: Check Lean files with: $CHECK <file.lean>\n120: It screens the file, elaborates it against the real definitions, and prints each theorem's axioms. You work inside a sandbox: this directory is writable, the Lean project and Mathlib are read-only, and ordinary shell commands are available. Never run lake. Scratch files may be created in this directory.\n121: \n122: Hard rules — a result that breaks any of them is discarded:\n123: - Change nothing above the first \\`theorem\\` line (imports, options, \\`Candidate\\`). Put helper lemmas after \\`Candidate\\`, as top-level theorem/lemma/def.\n124: - Keep the chosen theorem's name and header exactly; leave the other theorem untouched, with its sorry.\n125: - No sorry, admit, axiom, native_decide, namespace, section, variable, notation, macro, syntax, IO, or any metap